  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1102 AM MDT Mon Jul 20 2026\n\nA stagnant high-pressure system remains parked over the area.\nThis is leading to many rinse and repeat days when it comes to\nthe current forecast. Shower and thunderstorm chances remain\neach afternoon and evening, at least for the first half of the\nweek. Shower activity appears to become more widespread by\nTuesday and Wednesday as a shortwave rotates around the high\ncausing it to move to our southeast. This will allow PWATs to \nincrease to upwards of 200% of normal. Primary threats continue \nto be heavy rainfall, lightning, and gusty outflow winds (35-45 \nmph). Small hail is also a possibility. With that being said, \ndepending on where these storms choose to materialize, they \nintroduce a threat for flash flooding and debris flows. This \nremains a concern for areas with vulnerable terrain, including \nrecent burn scars in addition to burn scars from previous years.\nFor greater detail on the flash flooding risk, see the \nhydrology section below. \n\nBy midweek, the high-pressure system will shift slightly,\naltering our storm tracks. While storms have recently been\ntaking an unusual east-to-west path, the new pattern will likely\nsteer them up from the south and perhaps the west by late week.\nAfter that shortwave passes to our north westerly flow becomes\nestablished. This may alter the moisture across the area, which\nin turn may change daily storm chances.  \n\nAs far as temperatures are concerned, daytime highs will\ncontinue to be above normal for the next few days, with near\nnormal temperatures expected Wednesday and beyond. \n\nA special note for outdoor enthusiasts: Please continue to\nmonitor the forecast and adjust your plans as needed. These\nstorms can develop rapidly, bringing sudden hazards. Your best\nbet is to head out early in the morning, as storm activity\ntypically strengthens in the afternoon. Always ensure you have\nmultiple ways to receive weather warnings.",
